  "notes": "The baroque-emo producer — strings and theatre inside pop-punk: 88–150 straight with the backbeat on 2&4, A minor and other natural minors over i–VI–III–VII with chromatic passing chords. Three drum deltas are quoted: \"kick 1 and 3\", \"snare 2&4 (rimshot as an alternative)\" — the rim lane — and \"cymbal chokes and marching-snare figures as transitions\" with \"whole sections with no drums\", which is the crash/tom perc block, a 16th snare-roll ladder and a `dropOutBeats` of 0.94. The production fingerprint is a bass one and the research names it as such: \"played bass on roots **doubled by a pizzicato cello** — the string bass is part of the rhythm section, not a pad; that doubling is the production's fingerprint\". A note generator says that as a bass that is neither kick-locked nor free — `independent_riff` at 0.86 with a low anticipation and a staccato-friendly narrow passing-tone set — while the pizzicato violin and cello carry the counter as arp and answer-lick. He produced, recorded and mixed the flagship himself, which is why the model is a single coherent tone rather than a split. Melody C4–A5 with a wide theatrical contour. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research rates it high-medium. ⚠ Every drum probability here is **INFERRED**, as is `bpm.mode` 120; the 3/4 waltz sections the entry names are outside a 4/4 generator and are recorded here instead.",
